with trigger, horizon, expected move, invalidation, position-risk suggestion, and subsequent scoreboard. “Fast money” then means short feedback loop and favorable asymmetry, not merely “take more risk.”

For the commercial version, your instinct to sell the instruments plus packaged intelligence rather than only opaque calls is probably the stronger initial business. A broker/institution product could contain:

* authenticated source data and provenance;
* derived deltas/features;
* structural-rhyme and anomaly signals;
* historical analogues;
* scenario distributions;
* “here’s what this instrument is useful for” recipes;
* API access and the Atlas/Realitsea interface;
* a permanent prediction-and-calibration ledger.

There is an important regulatory boundary here. FINRA already describes firms using AI to identify patterns and potential price movements, and it specifically treats interactive tools that simulate investment outcomes as “investment analysis tools.” Once a broker-dealer uses AI to make securities recommendations to retail customers, Regulation Best Interest can apply, along with supervision and communications requirements.  So B2B analytical infrastructure first is a sensible architecture—not because it magically exempts the company from regulation, but because “here is a high-quality instrument and authenticated dataset” is structurally different from immediately becoming a personalized retail adviser.

